WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.067
Gradle's build cache is switched on.

2
00:00:05.067 --> 00:00:06.382
You run clean build.

3
00:00:06.382 --> 00:00:10.656
Clean deletes the build folders first, every compiled file, gone.

4
00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:14.273
So compileKotlin has to compile everything again.

5
00:00:14.273 --> 00:00:14.690
Right?

6
00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.149
The outputs are gone.

7
00:00:16.149 --> 00:00:19.277
Keep your answer, we'll check it in a minute.

8
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.773
Picture our bakery on its busiest morning.

9
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.697
The oven went cold overnight, and takes forty minutes to heat.

10
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:32.005
The prep list is written again from scratch, though the orders are the same as yesterday.

11
00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:37.659
Each branch makes its own croissant dough, though the main branch made the same dough an hour ago.

12
00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:43.143
And the van carries the whole kitchen, mixer included, to deliver one box of bread.

13
00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:45.208
None of these looks serious.

14
00:00:45.208 --> 00:00:48.167
Each costs a few minutes, so nobody fixes it.

15
00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:50.008
It's never one big disaster.

16
00:00:50.008 --> 00:00:52.375
It's four small ones, holding hands.

17
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:58.294
Gradle has four separate ways to skip repeated work, and they're easy to mix up.

18
00:00:58.294 --> 00:01:00.353
So let's give each one a picture.

19
00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:07.208
Up-to-date checks, from last time, skip a task whose inputs and outputs didn't change, in the same folder.

20
00:01:07.208 --> 00:01:08.900
The sauce still in the pot.

21
00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:17.803
The build cache copies a task's outputs from an earlier run anywhere, even another folder or another machine, when the inputs match.

22
00:01:17.803 --> 00:01:18.853
A shared fridge.

23
00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:23.829
The configuration cache skips running the build scripts when they didn't change.

24
00:01:23.829 --> 00:01:25.139
Yesterday's prep list.

25
00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:32.262
And the daemon is a background program Gradle keeps running between builds, so Gradle itself starts fast.

26
00:01:32.262 --> 00:01:33.444
An oven kept warm.

27
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:37.343
The build cache saves the most work, so let's open the fridge.

28
00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:40.307
Take one task, compileKotlin.

29
00:01:40.307 --> 00:01:45.057
Its inputs are the source files, the classpath, and the compiler flags.

30
00:01:45.267 --> 00:01:51.951
Before it runs, Gradle hashes all of those inputs into one cache key, a label made of their fingerprints.

31
00:01:52.167 --> 00:01:57.784
The fridge holds earlier outputs, each one filed under the key of the inputs that made it.

32
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.784
If the fridge already has that key, Gradle copies the outputs in and prints FROM-CACHE.

33
00:02:03.784 --> 00:02:04.648
No compiling.

34
00:02:04.867 --> 00:02:07.763
Notice what's not in the key: the folder path.

35
00:02:07.763 --> 00:02:11.792
So a fresh clone, in another folder, can still find its outputs.

36
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.426
Back to our question.

37
00:02:13.426 --> 00:02:15.191
Clean deleted the outputs.

38
00:02:15.191 --> 00:02:19.807
Pause and guess: does compileKotlin compile, or come from the cache?

39
00:02:22.033 --> 00:02:22.726
From cache.

40
00:02:22.726 --> 00:02:25.999
It isn't up to date, since its outputs were deleted.

41
00:02:25.999 --> 00:02:30.154
But its inputs didn't change, so their key is still in the fridge.

42
00:02:30.154 --> 00:02:33.239
Gradle restores the outputs instead of compiling.

43
00:02:33.467 --> 00:02:35.009
So that's the difference.

44
00:02:35.009 --> 00:02:37.725
Up-to-date needs the sauce still in the pot.

45
00:02:37.725 --> 00:02:41.551
The cache only needs the same recipe and the same ingredients.

46
00:02:42.467 --> 00:02:43.906
The lesson tried it.

47
00:02:43.906 --> 00:02:53.552
A fresh clone of hello-jvm, in a new folder on the same laptop, built with twenty actionable tasks: eight executed, twelve from cache.

48
00:02:53.767 --> 00:02:58.129
Many teams also run a remote cache, a server every build can read.

49
00:02:58.129 --> 00:03:00.905
That's the fridge shared between branches.

50
00:03:01.133 --> 00:03:06.666
The Gradle docs recommend that CI fills it from clean builds, and laptops only read.

51
00:03:06.666 --> 00:03:10.619
So half finished local changes never reach the shared cache.

52
00:03:10.833 --> 00:03:14.609
Pull main in the morning, and most of it is already compiled.

53
00:03:14.609 --> 00:03:16.713
Nobody makes the same dough twice.

54
00:03:17.633 --> 00:03:20.867
One more way to skip work, from the Kotlin plugin.

55
00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:25.070
Remember the two projects: app uses greeting, our little library.

56
00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:27.787
When greeting changes, must app recompile?

57
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.656
The lesson changed greeting in three ways.

58
00:03:30.656 --> 00:03:31.858
One: add a comment.

59
00:03:31.858 --> 00:03:33.629
Only greeting's compile ran.

60
00:03:33.629 --> 00:03:38.245
It produced the same class files, so everything in app stayed up to date.

61
00:03:38.467 --> 00:03:40.588
Three: add a public function.

62
00:03:40.588 --> 00:03:42.491
Now app's compile ran too.

63
00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:45.566
Two: change the code inside a function.

64
00:03:45.566 --> 00:03:46.742
Pause and guess.

65
00:03:46.742 --> 00:03:48.139
Does app recompile?

66
00:03:50.367 --> 00:03:50.571
No.

67
00:03:50.571 --> 00:03:52.753
app's compile stayed up to date.

68
00:03:52.753 --> 00:04:00.797
greeting's jar and the tests ran, but app compiles only against greeting's public signatures, and those didn't change.

69
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.503
That set of public signatures is called the ABI, the application binary interface.

70
00:04:06.503 --> 00:04:11.133
A change behind a stable public API doesn't ripple through the build.

71
00:04:11.133 --> 00:04:13.884
One more reason to prefer implementation.

72
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.233
Now take this to CI, where every runner starts fresh.

73
00:04:18.233 --> 00:04:24.904
The setup-gradle action caches libraries, the wrapper's Gradle, and the local build cache between runs.

74
00:04:25.133 --> 00:04:27.840
Two traps, because more caching sounds safer.

75
00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.606
Don't add another cache for the Gradle folder.

76
00:04:30.606 --> 00:04:32.471
It conflicts with setup-gradle.

77
00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:34.765
And don't run clean build in CI.

78
00:04:34.765 --> 00:04:42.313
On a fresh runner clean has nothing to delete, and on a reused machine it throws away work Gradle could have skipped.

79
00:04:42.533 --> 00:04:48.452
If you distrust a cache hit, the real fix is a task input that's missing from the build logic.

80
00:04:48.452 --> 00:04:53.363
And remember, for the configuration cache, a miss is only slower, never wrong.

81
00:04:54.267 --> 00:04:55.227
So, the moral.

82
00:04:55.227 --> 00:04:57.559
Same inputs give the same outputs.

83
00:04:57.559 --> 00:05:02.841
Label finished work by what went into it, and anyone, anywhere, can reuse it.

84
00:05:03.067 --> 00:05:05.163
The van with the whole kitchen in it?

85
00:05:05.163 --> 00:05:08.221
That's packing the image, and the lesson does it next.

86
00:05:08.433 --> 00:05:09.244
Quick check.

87
00:05:09.244 --> 00:05:13.767
You clone the project into a brand new folder, cache on, and build.

88
00:05:13.767 --> 00:05:16.468
Will tasks be up to date, or from cache?

89
00:05:16.468 --> 00:05:17.548
Pause and think.

90
00:05:19.767 --> 00:05:23.076
From cache, for every task the fridge already holds.

91
00:05:23.076 --> 00:05:26.068
Up-to-date checks only work in the same folder.

92
00:05:26.068 --> 00:05:27.723
The fridge works anywhere.
